  "answer": "This feature request has been filed before. It asks for a way to make any window transparent, with adjustable opacity, that doesn't depend on Always On Top. The team closes these requests as duplicates of PowerToys#1084 (\"Make windows Acrylic / Transparent\"), so that is where it is tracked. PowerToys has no such feature today. Always On Top has no opacity control either. Add a thumbs-up and your use case (a transparent window that doesn't stay on top) to #1084. The tickets don't offer a built-in workaround. Other commenters mention the third-party tools MenuTools and Glass2k for per-window transparency."
 },
